            <!-- Rich Text Body -->
            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'zh'">
                <p>做 Meta 广告投放，最怕遇到一种情况：素材数据看起来很漂亮——点击率 2%、CTR 高出行业均值 50%，但跑了三周，一单都没出。</p>
<p>这不是素材的问题，是你没搞清楚两个概念：<strong>能跑 ≠ 能卖</strong>。</p>
<p>「能跑」指素材能拿到曝光、有点击、 CPM 低、花钱顺利。「能卖」指素材能带来转化行为——加购、注册、付款。两者有时候重叠，但大多数时候是分开的。下面这套 SOP，专门用来做素材复盘时做出准确判断。</p>
<hr />
<h2>一、复盘前准备：拉取正确的数据组合</h2>
<p>在做任何判断前，先把数据拉对。很多人复盘只看 CTR，这是不对的。</p>
<p><strong>必须拉取的 5 个核心指标：</strong></p>
<ol>
<li>CPM（千次曝光成本）</li>
<li>CTR（点击率）</li>
<li>CPC（单次点击成本）</li>
<li>CVR（转化率，转化数/点击数）</li>
<li>CPA（单次转化成本）</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>数据来源建议：</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>用 Meta Ads Manager 导出原始数据</li>
<li>配合 Google Analytics 或 AppsFlyer 做转化归因</li>
<li>素材维度拆开看，不要用广告层级汇总数据</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>提醒：</strong> 如果你只看转化数，不看 CPM 和 CTR，素材能跑多久、跑多贵你根本不知道。</p>
</blockquote>
<hr />
<h2>二、判断素材是否「能跑」：5 项快速自检</h2>
<p>在评估转化能力前，先看素材的「跑量能力」。</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>检查项</th>
<th>合格标准</th>
<th>说明</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>CPM</td>
<td>低于 Campaign 平均 20% 以上</td>
<td>低于均值说明系统愿意推</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>CTR</td>
<td>高于行业基准或账户均值</td>
<td>低于基准说明素材不被感兴趣</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>CPC</td>
<td>不超过目标 CPA 的 30%</td>
<td>CPC 太贵，转化成本必然高</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>曝光稳定性</td>
<td>连续 3 天曝光量波动 &lt; 30%</td>
<td>大幅波动说明素材生命周期短</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>受众匹配度</td>
<td>频次在 3-5 次区间</td>
<td>频次太低说明受众窄，太高说明疲劳</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>如果 5 项全部合格 → 素材「能跑」，进入下一步。</strong></p>
<p><strong>如果 3 项以上不合格 → 素材跑量能力差，优先优化或暂停。</strong></p>
<hr />
<h2>三、判断素材是否「能卖」：3 步转化评估</h2>
<p>「能跑」只是门槛，「能卖」才是目标。用下面三步做转化能力评估。</p>
<h3>第一步：看漏斗断层</h3>
<p>对比 CTR 和 CVR 的变化趋势：</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>CTR 高 + CVR 低</strong> → 素材吸引眼球但不解决信任问题</li>
<li><strong>CTR 低 + CVR 高</strong> → 受众精准但素材钩子不够强</li>
<li><strong>两者都低</strong> → 素材和受众同时有问题</li>
</ul>
<p>某跨境电商团队测试发现，一款宠物喂食器的视频素材 CTR 达 3.2%，但 CVR 仅 0.4%。排查后发现问题：视频重点展示「智能定时」功能，但落地页在卖「大容量」，用户进来后找不到想要的东西，果断下架重做落地页匹配。</p>
<h3>第二步：对比同类素材的转化路径</h3>
<p>不要只看绝对值，要做横向对比。</p>
<ol>
<li>找出同类产品下 CVR 最高的 Top 3 素材</li>
<li>拆解它们的共性：卖点表达方式、CTA 文案、落地页结构</li>
<li>把 Top 3 的共性套用到表现差的素材上做 AB 测试</li>
</ol>
<h3>第三步：区分「素材问题」和「落地页问题」</h3>
<p><strong>能用落地页热图工具（如 Clarity、Hotjar）快速判断：</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>用户在素材卖点区域停留久，但在购买按钮区域跳出率高 → 落地页问题</li>
<li>用户在素材卖点区域跳出率高 → 素材钩子没钩住目标人群</li>
<li>两者都有问题 → 建议先修落地页，再优化素材</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>四、复盘 SOP 完整流程图</h2>
<p>把上面的内容浓缩成一个可打印的执行清单：</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>导出最近 7 天素材维度数据</strong></li>
<li><strong>自检 5 项跑量指标</strong> → 标记「能跑」和「不能跑」的素材</li>
<li><strong>针对「能跑」的素材做转化评估</strong> → 跳过不能跑的，避免浪费时间</li>
<li><strong>做漏斗断层分析</strong> → 判断问题是素材还是落地页</li>
<li><strong>横向对比 Top 转化素材</strong> → 提炼可复用元素</li>
<li><strong>制定优化计划</strong> → 落地页调整或素材迭代，分配测试预算</li>
<li><strong>7 天后复盘验证</strong> → 形成循环</li>
</ol>
<hr />
<h2>五、常见误区提醒</h2>
<ul>
<li>❌ 只看 CTR 高就判断素材好 → 很多高 CTR 素材是「标题党」，转化差</li>
<li>❌ 看到能跑就持续加预算 → 能跑不代表能卖，爆量后不转化才是大坑</li>
<li>❌ 素材和落地页分开优化 → 两者必须联动，任何一个拉胯都会死</li>
<li>❌ 只看短期数据（3 天内） → 转化行为有决策周期，建议看 7-14 天</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<p><strong>总结一下：</strong> 素材复盘时，先用 5 项指标确认「能跑」，再用漏斗分析判断「能卖」。能跑不代表值得加预算，能卖才是值得放量的信号。两个概念分清楚，你的投放效率会提升一大截。</p>
            </div>
            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <p>Running Meta ads and wondering why high-CTR creatives aren't converting? The key is distinguishing between creatives that <strong>run well</strong> (attract clicks) and those that <strong>sell well</strong> (drive conversions). This SOP gives your team a step-by-step framework for creative review.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Step 1: Gather the Right Data Combination</h2>
<p>Before evaluating, pull the complete metrics picture. Looking at CTR alone is insufficient.</p>
<p><strong>5 Core Metrics to Export:</strong></p>
<ol>
<li>CPM (Cost per 1,000 impressions)</li>
<li>CTR (Click-through rate)</li>
<li>CPC (Cost per click)</li>
<li>CVR (Conversion rate, conversions / clicks)</li>
<li>CPA (Cost per acquisition)</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>Data sources:</strong> Meta Ads Manager (raw data, creative-level breakdown), plus Google Analytics or AppsFlyer for conversion attribution.</p>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Note:</strong> Monitoring conversions only without CPM and CTR leaves you blind to how long and how expensively your creative will run.</p>
</blockquote>
<hr />
<h2>Step 2: Check If the Creative "Runs" — 5-Point Quick Scan</h2>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Checkpoint</th>
<th>Pass Standard</th>
<th>Notes</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>CPM</td>
<td>20%+ below campaign average</td>
<td>Below average = Meta's system愿意推送</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>CTR</td>
<td>Above industry benchmark or account average</td>
<td>Below benchmark = low interest signal</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>CPC</td>
<td>Under 30% of target CPA</td>
<td>Expensive clicks = expensive conversions</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Impression stability</td>
<td>&lt;30% fluctuation over 3 consecutive days</td>
<td>High volatility = short creative lifespan</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Frequency match</td>
<td>Between 3-5</td>
<td>Too low = narrow audience; too high = fatigue</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>All 5 pass → Creative "runs" → Proceed to Step 3.</strong></p>
<p><strong>3+ fail → Poor runability → Optimize or pause first.</strong></p>
<hr />
<h2>Step 3: Check If the Creative "Sells" — 3-Part Conversion Assessment</h2>
<h3>Part A: Identify Funnel Drop-offs</h3>
<p>Compare CTR and CVR trends:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>High CTR + Low CVR</strong> → Creative grabs attention but fails to build trust</li>
<li><strong>Low CTR + High CVR</strong> → Audience is right but the hook is weak</li>
<li><strong>Both low</strong> → Both creative and audience need work</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Case reference:</strong> A cross-border e-commerce team tested a pet feeder video with CTR at 3.2% but CVR at only 0.4%. Investigation revealed the video emphasized "smart timer" but the landing page promoted "large capacity." Users couldn't find what they came for — the landing page was rematched to the creative angle.</p>
<h3>Part B: Benchmark Against Peer Performers</h3>
<p>Don't evaluate in isolation — compare横向.</p>
<ol>
<li>Identify Top 3 CVR creatives for the same product</li>
<li>Break down common elements: value proposition style, CTA copy, landing page structure</li>
<li>Apply these patterns to underperforming creatives in A/B tests</li>
</ol>
<h3>Part C: Isolate Creative vs. Landing Page Issues</h3>
<p>Use heatmap tools (Microsoft Clarity, Hotjar) for quick diagnosis:</p>
<ul>
<li>Users linger on creative's selling point but bounce at purchase button → Landing page issue</li>
<li>Users bounce at creative's selling point → Creative hook doesn't resonate</li>
<li>Both issues present → Fix landing page first, then creative</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>Step 4: Full SOP Checklist</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Export last 7 days creative-level data</strong></li>
<li><strong>Run 5-point runability check</strong> → Tag "runnable" vs "not runnable"</li>
<li><strong>Evaluate conversion potential for "runnable" creatives only</strong> → Skip the rest to save time</li>
<li><strong>Analyze funnel drop-offs</strong> → Identify if issue is creative or landing page</li>
<li><strong>Cross-reference with top converter creatives</strong> → Extract reusable patterns</li>
<li><strong>Build optimization plan</strong> → Landing page tweaks or creative iterations, allocate test budget</li>
<li><strong>Re-evaluate after 7 days</strong> → Complete the loop</li>
</ol>
<hr />
<h2>Common Pitfalls to Avoid</h2>
<ul>
<li>❌ Judging a creative good just because CTR is high → Many high-CTR creatives are clickbait with poor conversion</li>
<li>❌ Adding budget to anything that "runs" → Runability doesn't guarantee sellability; scaling non-converters is costly</li>
<li>❌ Optimizing creative and landing page separately → Both must align; either one failing kills performance</li>
<li>❌ Relying on short-term data (under 3 days) → Conversion decisions take time; evaluate on 7-14 day windows</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<p><strong>Bottom line:</strong> In creative reviews, first verify "runability" with 5 metrics, then assess "sellability" through funnel analysis. "Runs well" doesn't mean "deserves more budget." Only "sells well" signals a creative worth scaling. When you separate these two concepts clearly, your campaign efficiency improves dramatically.</p>
